1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,463
The Indian Convicts Who
Built Colonial Singapore,

2
00:00:05,543 --> 00:00:06,900
1825 to 1873

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,834
On the 18th of April 1825
the brig Horatio reached

4
00:00:10,914 --> 00:00:13,966
Singapore with 80 convicts from Madras,

5
00:00:14,046 --> 00:00:19,425
transferred from the British penal
station at Bencoolen in Sumatra.

6
00:00:19,425 --> 00:00:24,425
A week later 122 more arrived from Bengal.

7
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:27,604
For the next 48 years
Singapore took in convicts

8
00:00:27,684 --> 00:00:30,659
sentenced to transportation
in British India,

9
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:34,665
and much of the early town,
from its roads to its cathedral

10
00:00:34,745 --> 00:00:38,275
and the Governor's house,
was built by their labour.

11
00:00:38,275 --> 00:00:43,198
Britain had been sending Indian
convicts to its settlements

12
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:47,353
in the region since before
Singapore was founded:

13
00:00:47,433 --> 00:00:52,775
to Bencoolen from 1787, Penang
from 1796 and Malacca from 1805.

14
00:00:52,775 --> 00:00:56,145
Singapore was short of
labour and grew faster than

15
00:00:56,225 --> 00:00:59,595
the other Straits Settlements,
and by the 1830s it

16
00:00:59,675 --> 00:01:04,424
had become the main convict station,
holding more than 2,000 convicts,

17
00:01:04,504 --> 00:01:06,850
over half of those in the Straits.

18
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:12,678
In 1857 the jail held
2,139 convicts from India,

19
00:01:12,758 --> 00:01:14,850
Burma and Ceylon.

20
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:17,588
According to J. F. A. McNair,

21
00:01:17,668 --> 00:01:20,989
who ran it from 1857 until the end,

22
00:01:21,069 --> 00:01:26,025
about 1,900 were under its
control at any one time.

23
00:01:26,025 --> 00:01:28,152
Most were ordinary criminals,

24
00:01:28,232 --> 00:01:32,875
sentenced by Indian courts to
transportation, often for life.

25
00:01:32,875 --> 00:01:38,145
McNair's count of the convicts
still in the Straits Settlements in

26
00:01:38,225 --> 00:01:43,171
1873 lists 581 transported for
dacoity, or armed gang robbery,

27
00:01:43,251 --> 00:01:48,926
269 for robbery with murder, 256 for
thuggee, the robbery and murder of

28
00:01:49,006 --> 00:01:54,275
travellers by organised gangs,
and 21 for professional poisoning.

29
00:01:54,275 --> 00:01:56,908
Most of the rest had been convicted

30
00:01:56,988 --> 00:01:59,700
of murder or robbery with violence.

31
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:03,025
A smaller number were political prisoners.

32
00:02:03,025 --> 00:02:06,180
After the uprising of 1857 in India,

33
00:02:06,260 --> 00:02:09,225
rebels were shipped to Singapore.

34
00:02:09,225 --> 00:02:13,669
The first group, 17 men
and 2 women who arrived on

35
00:02:13,749 --> 00:02:16,384
the Earl Grey in October 1857,

36
00:02:16,464 --> 00:02:22,627
included five described as mutineers,
and McNair recorded the arrival

37
00:02:22,707 --> 00:02:27,050
of a large number of
mutineers in 1858 and 1859.

38
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:31,514
The Sikh leader Bhai Maharaj
Singh, sent to Singapore in 1850,

39
00:02:31,594 --> 00:02:34,812
was a State prisoner,
held apart in a cell at

40
00:02:34,892 --> 00:02:38,850
Outram Road rather than put
to work with the convicts.

41
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:42,117
When the first convicts
landed, four free watchmen

42
00:02:42,197 --> 00:02:45,075
from Chittagong were
put in charge of them.

43
00:02:45,075 --> 00:02:49,750
The Resident, George Bonham, found
that the convicts worked willingly,

44
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:53,622
so he dismissed the paid
watchmen and chose five Madrasis

45
00:02:53,702 --> 00:02:58,050
and five Bengalis from among the
convicts to supervise the rest.

46
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:01,458
McNair thought this was
possibly the first time

47
00:03:01,538 --> 00:03:05,100
any penal establishment
had tried such a system.

48
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:10,183
As more convicts arrived, one convict
warder was appointed for every

49
00:03:10,263 --> 00:03:15,275
twenty men and paid $3 a month on
top of his rations and clothing.

50
00:03:15,275 --> 00:03:20,381
Under rules formalised by Governor
William Butterworth in 1845,

51
00:03:20,461 --> 00:03:24,000
the convicts were
divided into six classes.

52
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,575
New arrivals worked in light
irons in the fourth class,

53
00:03:27,655 --> 00:03:31,975
and those thought likely to escape
wore heavy irons in the fifth.

54
00:03:31,975 --> 00:03:36,669
After a probation, a convict moved
into the third class to work on

55
00:03:36,749 --> 00:03:41,299
roads and public works, and good
conduct could lift him into the

56
00:03:41,379 --> 00:03:46,225
second class as a petty officer,
hospital orderly or office worker.

57
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:48,271
At the top was the first class,

58
00:03:48,351 --> 00:03:52,591
trustworthy convicts on a
ticket-of-leave, who lived and worked

59
00:03:52,671 --> 00:03:56,100
outside the jail and
reported to a monthly muster.

60
00:03:56,100 --> 00:04:01,350
A man serving a life sentence
could reach it after sixteen years.

61
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:04,750
The convicts also built their own jail.

62
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:09,573
It took about twenty years, was
finished in 1860, and on completion

63
00:04:09,653 --> 00:04:13,305
was the largest building
complex in the settlement,

64
00:04:13,385 --> 00:04:18,800
stretching along Bras Basah Road from
Victoria Street to Bencoolen Street.

65
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,250
The convicts were not the first
Indians in that part of town.

66
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:27,576
Indian washermen, or dhobies,
had come in 1819 with the sepoy

67
00:04:27,656 --> 00:04:31,764
garrison at the foot of
Government Hill, now Fort Canning,

68
00:04:31,844 --> 00:04:36,250
as part of the service workers
who looked after the soldiers.

69
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:40,928
They were free workers, mostly from
what is now Uttar Pradesh and Bihar,

70
00:04:41,008 --> 00:04:44,099
along with Tamil dhobies
from the Madras region,

71
00:04:44,179 --> 00:04:48,791
and they washed clothes in the stream
that fed the Bras Basah canal and

72
00:04:48,871 --> 00:04:53,100
dried them on the green nearby,
which gave Dhoby Ghaut its name.

73
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:57,202
Singapore's early Indian
population grew from both groups,

74
00:04:57,282 --> 00:05:01,175
free workers and convicts,
living a few streets apart.

75
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:04,289
The convicts' first big
task was filling in the

76
00:05:04,369 --> 00:05:08,231
mud flat that became Commercial
Square, now Raffles Place,

77
00:05:08,311 --> 00:05:10,825
with earth carried from Pearl's Hill.

78
00:05:10,825 --> 00:05:16,219
From 1833, when the architect G. D.
Coleman became superintendent of convicts,

79
00:05:16,299 --> 00:05:19,376
they built roads: Beach
Road along the shore,

80
00:05:19,456 --> 00:05:24,008
Bukit Timah Road to carry goods
in from the interior, and Thomson,

81
00:05:24,088 --> 00:05:29,000
Serangoon and Bedok roads through
the forest and along the east coast.

82
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,615
From 1845 the superintendent
Henry Man turned

83
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:36,225
the jail into a training
ground for trades.

84
00:05:36,225 --> 00:05:40,203
Convicts learned carpentry,
bricklaying, blacksmithing,

85
00:05:40,283 --> 00:05:45,074
stonecutting and lime-burning, and
the bricks from their kilns off

86
00:05:45,154 --> 00:05:49,950
Serangoon Road won a silver medal
at the Agra Exhibition in 1867.

87
00:05:49,950 --> 00:05:53,240
Their best-known work is
Saint Andrew's Church,

88
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,400
now Saint Andrew's Cathedral.

89
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:59,964
Its designer, Ronald MacPherson,
chose a simple Gothic style

90
00:06:00,044 --> 00:06:05,075
that convict builders could manage,
and they finished it in 1862.

91
00:06:05,075 --> 00:06:10,264
They plastered its walls and columns
with Madras chunam, a mix of shell lime,

92
00:06:10,344 --> 00:06:14,643
egg white and coarse sugar that
was polished to a glossy finish,

93
00:06:14,723 --> 00:06:18,350
and boy convicts climbed
the spire to fix its slates.

94
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:21,775
Government House, now
the Istana, followed.

95
00:06:21,775 --> 00:06:25,821
When the Straits Settlements
passed from Indian to Crown

96
00:06:25,901 --> 00:06:30,462
rule on the 1st of April 1867,
the new Governor, Sir Harry Ord,

97
00:06:30,542 --> 00:06:34,300
had no official residence,
and McNair designed one.

98
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:37,738
According to McNair, convicts
did all the brickwork,

99
00:06:37,818 --> 00:06:40,377
the exterior plastering and most of the

100
00:06:40,457 --> 00:06:43,975
flooring and interior work,
and made all the bricks.

101
00:06:43,975 --> 00:06:47,179
The house was ready for the visit of

102
00:06:47,259 --> 00:06:50,725
the Duke of Edinburgh in October 1869.

103
00:06:50,725 --> 00:06:54,875
For the colony, the convicts
were a cheap source of labour.

104
00:06:54,875 --> 00:06:59,033
In the early years each convict
received rations, clothing,

105
00:06:59,113 --> 00:07:03,487
a blanket a year and 50 cents a
month for salt and condiments,

106
00:07:03,567 --> 00:07:06,800
and convict warders cost
less than free ones.

107
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:11,005
In 1849 the Resident Councillor,
Thomas Church, told the Governor

108
00:07:11,085 --> 00:07:15,091
that "the labour of the convicts
is equivalent to all expenses

109
00:07:15,171 --> 00:07:18,189
incurred in their
maintenance at this station",

110
00:07:18,269 --> 00:07:22,935
and argued that giving repairs and
minor works to the convict department

111
00:07:23,015 --> 00:07:27,300
would be "vastly more economical"
than the existing arrangements.

112
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,262
According to BiblioAsia,
the large public buildings

113
00:07:31,342 --> 00:07:35,066
put up with convict labour
also strengthened the

114
00:07:35,146 --> 00:07:38,950
colony's case for separation
from India in 1867.

115
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:42,725
Some convicts made careers
after their release.

116
00:07:42,725 --> 00:07:47,167
Hammapah, a convict land surveyor,
set up a land brokerage in the

117
00:07:47,247 --> 00:07:51,700
east of the island, and his son
Somapah carried on the business.

118
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:55,008
According to BiblioAsia,
Somapah Village off

119
00:07:55,088 --> 00:07:58,475
Upper Serangoon Road
took its name from him.

120
00:07:58,475 --> 00:08:02,851
Bawajee Rajaram, a convict from
Bombay, became Singapore's first

121
00:08:02,931 --> 00:08:06,819
local draughtsman and headed
the team that drew plans for

122
00:08:06,899 --> 00:08:11,425
Saint Andrew's Church, the General
Hospital and Government House.

123
00:08:11,425 --> 00:08:15,432
After his release he worked
on municipal drawings,

124
00:08:15,512 --> 00:08:19,925
and in 1890 he designed a
bungalow on Devonshire Road.

125
00:08:19,925 --> 00:08:23,816
Transportation to
Singapore officially ended

126
00:08:23,896 --> 00:08:27,427
with the transfer to Crown rule in 1867,

127
00:08:27,507 --> 00:08:32,200
but the convict establishment
carried on until 1873.

128
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:36,645
On the 3rd of May 1873 the
Straits Times Overland Journal

129
00:08:36,725 --> 00:08:41,409
reported that the steamer Paknam
had been chartered "for the

130
00:08:41,489 --> 00:08:46,570
lump sum of $16,000" to take the
convicts to the Andaman Islands,

131
00:08:46,650 --> 00:08:51,175
where India had opened a new
penal settlement after 1857.

132
00:08:51,175 --> 00:08:53,921
The last convicts left on the

133
00:08:54,001 --> 00:08:56,925
Paknam on the 8th of May 1873.

134
00:08:56,925 --> 00:09:00,650
Those on a ticket-of-leave
were allowed to stay.

135
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:04,097
McNair wrote that they
merged into the population,

136
00:09:04,177 --> 00:09:07,553
"continuing to earn their
livelihood as artizans,

137
00:09:07,633 --> 00:09:10,525
cow keepers, cart drivers, and the like".

138
00:09:10,525 --> 00:09:13,533
The old and infirm were kept in Singapore

139
00:09:13,613 --> 00:09:16,250
at the Indian government's expense.

140
00:09:16,250 --> 00:09:18,399
Where it fits in the bigger story:

141
00:09:18,479 --> 00:09:22,464
Singapore's early public buildings
are usually credited to the

142
00:09:22,544 --> 00:09:25,625
architects and governors
who commissioned them.

143
00:09:25,625 --> 00:09:28,642
Much of the labour came
from Indian convicts,

144
00:09:28,722 --> 00:09:33,116
most of them transported for
serious crimes, who worked their way

145
00:09:33,196 --> 00:09:37,050
up through a system run largely
by prisoners themselves.

146
00:09:37,050 --> 00:09:40,814
Many stayed on after 1873,
and along with the free

147
00:09:40,894 --> 00:09:43,888
migrants who came before and after them,

148
00:09:43,968 --> 00:09:48,350
they were part of how Singapore's
Indian community began.
